<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">

<head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0" />
    @* Declared here as well as in the stylesheet, because this is the earliest point
       a browser — or an extension deciding whether a site needs its help — can read
       it. AniQueue ships a full dark palette, so anything re-theming it on top is
       redoing work, and in one measured case broke the page outright (§9). *@
    <meta name="color-scheme" content="light dark" />
    <base href="/" />
    @* Without an explicit icon, browsers probe /favicon.ico on every page load.
       That 404s, and the 404 handler re-executes the whole app — returning a 4.6 KB
       HTML page in answer to a request for an icon. *@
    <link rel="icon" href="@Assets["favicon.svg"]" type="image/svg+xml" />
    <ResourcePreloader />
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="@Assets["app.css"]" />
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="@Assets["AniQueue.Web.styles.css"]" />
    <ImportMap />

    @* A real title in the markup, because HeadOutlet no longer prerenders one and a
       tab labelled with the URL for the first moments of every load is worse than a
       name that is occasionally one page behind. Page-specific titles replace it as
       soon as the circuit renders. *@
    <title>AniQueue</title>

    @* Both roots render only in the circuit, never into the served HTML.
       (See ROADMAP §9.) The two errors that killed the circuit on load —
       "insertBefore … parentNode is null" and "No element is currently associated
       with component 1" — both come from Blazor binding to server-rendered markup a
       dark-mode extension had rewritten in between. Prerendering is what creates
       that markup, and the binding step is what fails; without it the components
       render into an empty document and there is nothing to reconcile.

       The cost is honest and it is paid on every load: nothing is visible until the
       circuit connects. That is a fraction of a second on the local network this is
       built for, and the loading shell below covers it. *@
    <HeadOutlet @rendermode="@(new InteractiveServerRenderMode(prerender: false))" />
</head>

<body>
    @* Shown until the circuit renders the application over it. Hidden by CSS rather
       than by script, so it cannot outlive the thing it is covering if scripting
       fails halfway. *@
    <div class="app-loading" aria-hidden="true">AniQueue</div>

    <Routes @rendermode="@(new InteractiveServerRenderMode(prerender: false))" />
    <ReconnectModal />
    <script src="@Assets["_framework/blazor.web.js"]"></script>
</body>

</html>
